index 7d17e35..f98d33c 100644
--- a/docs/src/usage/fitter.rst
+++ b/docs/src/usage/fitter.rst
@@ -109,11 +109,13 @@ Simulated annealing with anneal
 ----------------------------------
 
 :py:meth:`~chemfit.fitter.Fitter.fit_anneal` runs the
-`anneal <https://anneal.rgoswami.me>`_ drivers. It starts at
-``initial_params``, never evaluates outside ``bounds`` (every leaf needs a
-finite ``(lower, upper)`` pair), calls ``step()`` after each evaluation, and
-evaluates the objective at most ``budget`` times. Array leaves are supported,
-so atomic positions can be optimized directly:
+`anneal <https://anneal.rgoswami.me>`_ drivers (install the ``anneal`` extra,
+which requires anneal 0.10.0 or later). It evaluates ``initial_params`` first,
+never evaluates outside ``bounds`` (every leaf needs a finite
+``(lower, upper)`` pair, and equal bounds hold a coordinate fixed), calls
+``step()`` after each evaluation, and evaluates the objective exactly
+``budget`` times. It returns the best parameters evaluated in this fit. Array
+leaves are supported, so atomic positions can be optimized directly:
 
 .. code-block:: python
 
@@ -125,15 +127,21 @@ so atomic positions can be optimized directly:
         initial_params={"positions": initial_positions},  # shape (n_atoms, 3)
         bounds={"positions": (-3.0, 3.0)},
     )
-    opt_params = fitter.fit_anneal(budget=10_000, method="boltzmann")
+    opt_params = fitter.fit_anneal(budget=10_000, method="gsa")
 
 ``method="portfolio"`` (the default) is anneal's general-purpose driver;
 ``"boltzmann"``, ``"fast"`` and ``"gsa"`` are the classical simulated
-annealing presets and ``"gpmd"`` is gap-proportional Metropolis descent.
+annealing presets and ``"gpmd"`` is gap-proportional Metropolis descent. On a
+smooth fit, such as thirteen Lennard-Jones atoms from random positions,
+``"gsa"`` and ``"gpmd"`` reach far lower losses than the default at 2000 and
+10000 evaluations (see the
+`anneal ChemFit guide <https://anneal.rgoswami.me/howto/chemfit.html>`_).
 Steps are taken in units of each parameter's box width; pass ``preset=`` (for
-example ``anneal.Boltzmann(t_init=0.5, sigma=0.01)``) to set the step and the
-temperature, which is in loss units. An objective exception is re-raised once
-anneal returns, unless the fitter swallows exceptions.
+example ``anneal.Boltzmann(t_init=0.5, sigma=0.01)`` with
+``method="boltzmann"``) to set the step and the temperature, which is in loss
+units. Exceptions from the objective, from callbacks run by ``step()`` and
+from ``gradient=`` follow ``swallow_exceptions`` and ``log_exceptions``; when
+one is re-raised, the fit stops at that evaluation.
 
 ----------------------------------
 Objective interface
